<section class="section pain-drag-section pain-clean-section" data-render-deferred="true" id="growth-pain">
<div class="page-shell pain-clean-layout">
<header class="pain-clean-heading" data-reveal="">
<p class="eyebrow">The hidden cost of growth</p>
<h2 class="section-title">What happens when every new client adds more manual work?</h2>
<p class="section-copy">
            Another store. Another catalog. Another approval chain. Another set of purchase orders,
            invoices, and shipping updates your team has to chase.
          </p>
</header>
<div aria-label="An astronaut pushing against a leaning wall of manual work" class="pain-clean-visual pain-clean-visual--illustration" data-reveal="">
<div class="pain-clean-illustration-frame">
<img alt="Illustration of an astronaut pushing a leaning stack labeled artwork approvals, supplier coordination, purchase orders, status chasing, and manual follow up." decoding="async" fetchpriority="low" height="645" loading="lazy" src="data:image/webp;base64," width="860"/>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div aria-label="Operational scalability questions" class="pain-question-grid">
<article class="pain-question-card" data-reveal="">
<span>01</span>
<h3>How much time does your team spend moving the same order between systems?</h3>
<p>Orders, artwork, purchase orders, invoices, and tracking should not require repeated manual entry.</p>
</article>
<article class="pain-question-card" data-reveal="">
<span>02</span>
<h3>What happens when the person who understands the workflow is unavailable?</h3>
<p>When the process lives inside someone’s head, growth creates dependency instead of resilience.</p>
</article>
<article class="pain-question-card" data-reveal="">
<span>03</span>
<h3>Could your operation handle 20 more company stores without adding more people?</h3>
<p>If every new program adds the same administration, the business is growing, but the operation is not scaling.</p>
</article>
</div>
</section>
